'''Alan McCrory''' '''Wilson''' (born '''Alan McCrory'''; July 16, 1973) is an American lawyer, politician, and military officer who has served as the 51st [[South Carolina Attorney General|attorney general of South Carolina]] since 2011. He is a member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]].

The adopted son of U.S. Representative [[Joe Wilson]], he attended [[Francis Marion University]] and the [[Joseph F. Rice School of Law|University of South Carolina School of Law]]. He serves as a [[colonel]] in the [[South Carolina National Guard]] and worked as an attorney in [[Columbia, South Carolina|Columbia]] before being elected attorney general in [[2010 South Carolina elections|2010]]. As attorney general, Wilson has litigated to block [[same-sex marriage]], invalidate the [[Affordable Care Act]], challenge environmental regulations, defend anti-abortion laws, and prohibit masking and vaccine requirements. He has advocated against cannabis decriminalization and [[Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals]].

Wilson is the Republican nominee for [[governor of South Carolina]] in the [[2026 South Carolina gubernatorial election|2026 election]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=2026-06-23 |title=Alan Wilson wins GOP nomination for South Carolina governor after dual Trump endorsement |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/south-carolina-governor-runoff-election-winner-wilson-rcna351122 |access-date=2026-06-23 |website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
Wilson was born Alan McCrory on July 16, 1973 in [[Fort Wolters]], [[Texas]].<ref name="manual2012">{{cite book |title=2012 South Carolina Legislative Manual |chapter=State Officers |chapter-url=http://scstatehouse.gov/man12/40_StateOff.pdf |page=342 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120511150942/http://scstatehouse.gov/man12/40_StateOff.pdf |archive-date=May 11, 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="manual2025">{{cite book |title=2025 South Carolina Legislative Manual |editor-last=Reid |editor-first=Charles F. |chapter=State Officers |chapter-url=https://www.scstatehouse.gov/man25/38_StateOfficers.pdf |page=345 |access-date=October 11, 2026}}</ref> His father, Michael Alan McCrory, was an Army captain and [[Vietnam veteran]] who died in a military training exercise at [[Fort Bragg]] when Wilson was two years old.<ref name="Joe Iraq">{{cite news | url=http://www.lowcountrynewspapers.net/archive/node/70300 | title=Congressman with military ties backs Iraq war | work=[[The Island Packet]] | date=February 15, 2004 | access-date=June 19, 2011 | author=Simon, Darran | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110723135528/http://www.lowcountrynewspapers.net/archive/node/70300 | archive-date=2011-07-23 | url-status=dead }}</ref> His mother, Roxanne Dusenbury McCrory, later married [[Joe Wilson]], who currently serves as the [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representative]] for {{ushr|SC|2}}. Joe Wilson adopted Alan when he was three years old, and Alan subsequently took his surname.<ref name="Roig-Franzia">{{cite news | url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2010/06/20/AR2010062003065.html | title=Joe Wilson's stepson vs. insurance mogul's son-in-law in South Carolina runoff | newspaper=The Washington Post | date=June 21, 2010 | access-date=June 19, 2011 | author=Roig-Franzia, Manuel}}</ref> Along with his three brothers, Alan is an [[Eagle Scout (Boy Scouts of America)|Eagle Scout]].<ref name="campaign about">{{cite web | last1=Wilson | first1=Alan | url=http://www.wilsonforag.com/about/ | title=MEET ALAN WILSON | work=Alan Wilson for Attorney General | access-date=June 19, 2011}}</ref>

Wilson graduated from [[Francis Marion University]], where he joined [[Pi Kappa Alpha]] through the Theta Delta chapter, in 1996 with a [[Bachelor of Science]] in [[political science]].<ref name="manual2025" /><ref name="statewide candidate biographies">{{cite web|url=http://www.thestate.com/2010/10/28/1533509/2010-statewide-candidate-biographies.html |title=2010 statewide candidate biographies |publisher=McClatchy Company |work=[[The State (newspaper)|The State]] |date=October 28, 2010 |access-date=June 19, 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120813004653/http://www.thestate.com/2010/10/28/1533509/2010-statewide-candidate-biographies.html |archive-date=August 13, 2012 }}</ref> He later received a [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of South Carolina School of Law]] in 2002.<ref name="WORD">{{cite web|url=http://www.newsradioword.com/pages/6304665.php |title=Alan Wilson: Republican Candidate for Attorney General |publisher=Entercom Communications |work=[[WORD (AM)|News Radio WORD]] |access-date=June 19, 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110929111132/http://www.newsradioword.com/pages/6304665.php |archive-date=September 29, 2011 }}</ref>

Wilson joined the South Carolina National Guard in 1996 after finishing his undergraduate studies.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://wilsonforsc.com/about-me |title=About me - Alan Wilson For Governor |access-date=June 9, 2026}}</ref> He currently serves as a colonel in the [[Judge Advocate General]] Corps.<ref name="manual2025" />

==Legal career==
Wilson interned in the South Carolina Attorney General's office under [[Charlie Condon]]. After law school, he worked for Judge Marc H. Westbrook of the [[South Carolina Circuit Court]]. Wilson later served as an assistant solicitor and as an assistant attorney general in South Carolina. In 2009, he moved to the private sector and started working at the law firm Willoughby & Hoefer in [[Columbia, South Carolina]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Meet the Attorney General |url=https://www.scag.gov/about-the-office/meet-the-attorney-general/ |publisher=South Carolina Attorney General's Office |access-date=October 11, 2026}}</ref>

==Political career==
===Attorney General of South Carolina===
[[File:Nikki Haley (15334571143).jpg|thumb|Wilson speaking alongside Governor [[Nikki Haley]]]]
[[Henry McMaster]] did not run for re-election as Attorney General, choosing instead to run for governor to succeed term-limited incumbent [[Mark Sanford]].<ref name="priorities">{{cite web|url=http://www.thestate.com/2011/01/30/1670634/new-attorney-general-sets-priorities.html |title=New attorney general sets priorities |publisher=McClatchy Company |work=[[The State (newspaper)|The State]] |date=January 30, 2011 |access-date=June 19, 2011 |author=Monk, John |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110205210334/http://www.thestate.com/2011/01/30/1670634/new-attorney-general-sets-priorities.html |archive-date=February 5, 2011 }}</ref> Wilson won the Republican nomination in a [[runoff election]] on June 22, 2010, receiving 60 percent of the vote against his opponent Leighton Lord.<ref name="runoff">{{cite news | url=http://chronicle.augusta.com/news/government/elections/2010-06-22/joe-wilsons-son-wins-runoff-ag?v=1277253517 | title=Joe Wilson's son wins runoff for AG | work=[[Associated Press]] | date=June 22, 2010 | agency=Associated Press | access-date=June 19, 2011}}</ref> Wilson defeated Democratic nominee Matthew Richardson in the general election.<ref name="Glenn Smith">{{cite news | title=Wilson easily wins AG post in 1st bid | work=The Post and Courier | date=November 3, 2010 | author=Smith, Glenn | location=Charleston, SC}}</ref>

Wilson was re-elected in 2014, defeating the Democratic nominee, Parnell Diggs.<ref>Lillia Callum-Penso, [https://www.greenvilleonline.com/story/news/local/2014/11/04/alan-wilson-wins-sc-attorney-general-race/18502349/ Alan Wilson wins SC attorney general race], ''Greenville News'' (November 5, 2014).</ref>

Wilson was elected to a third term in 2018. In the contentious Republican primary, Wilson faced two challengers for renomination, state Representative [[Todd Atwater]] and attorney William Herlong of [[Greenville, South Carolina|Greenville]].<ref name=Cranney>Joseph Cranney, [https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/ag-alan-wilson-wins-gop-nomination-overcomes-contentious-primary/article_a990a202-7963-11e8-b21a-a706c40a538b.html AG Alan Wilson wins GOP nomination, overcomes contentious primary], ''Post & Courier'' (June 26, 2018).</ref> Both challengers focused on ethics issues, criticizing Wilson for his connections to the Quinns, who reached a plea deal after being embroiled in a Statehouse corruption investigation.<ref name=Adcox>Seanna Adcox, [https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/alan-wilson-wins-3rd-term-as-scs-top-prosecutor/article_5e98e3e8-da04-11e8-91ee-f7234556d937.html Alan Wilson wins 3rd term as SC's top prosecutor], ''Post & Courier''.</ref> In the initial Republican primary, Wilson received 48.6%, Atwater received 29.7%, and Herlong received almost 22%; because no candidate received a majority, the nomination was decided by a primary runoff election.<ref name=Cranney/><ref name=Smith>Tim Smith, [https://www.greenvilleonline.com/story/news/local/south-carolina/2018/06/20/sc-attorney-general-runoff-incumbent-vs-challenger-alan-wilson-todd-atwater/717521002/ GOP runoff for South Carolina Attorney General: Alan Wilson vs. Todd Atwater], ''The Greenville News'' (June 20, 2018).</ref> Although Herlong endorsed Atwater in the runoff,<ref name=Smith/> Wilson won renomination, receiving about 65% of the vote to Atwater's 35%.<ref name=Cranney/> In the general election, Wilson received about 55% of the vote, defeating Democratic nominee Constance Anastopoulo.<ref name=Adcox/> Wilson ranked number 1 on the [[The Post and Courier|Post and Courier]] Columbia's 2025 Power List.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=Post and Courier Columbia |date=2025-12-16 |title=The Post and Courier Columbia's 2025 Power List: 30 people getting stuff done in the capital city |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/columbia/news/post-and-courier-columbia-power-list-2025/article_5a3e81fd-fee8-4463-a04d-30446c46945d.html |access-date=2025-12-17 |website=Post and Courier |language=en}}</ref>

=== 2026 South Carolina gubernatorial campaign ===
{{Main|2026 South Carolina gubernatorial election}}
On June 23, 2025, Wilson announced his candidacy for governor.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Reynolds |first=Nick |date=2025-06-23 |title=Attorney General Alan Wilson officially enters governor's race, kicking off battle of 2026 |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/alan-wilson-announces-governor-south-carolina-republican/article_aa3496b4-e9d5-4a79-9421-94970ec67081.html |access-date=2025-06-24 |website=Post and Courier |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Green |first=Mary |date=2025-06-23 |title=SC AG Alan Wilson launches campaign for governor |url=https://www.wistv.com/2025/06/23/sc-ag-alan-wilson-making-special-announcement-amid-expected-gubernatorial-campaign/ |access-date=2025-06-24 |website=WIS-TV |language=en}}</ref> He and his running mate, state Senator [[Mike Reichenbach (politician) |Mike Reichenbach]],<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.fitsnews.com/2026/01/09/crossroads-2026-alan-wilson-taps-s-c-senator-as-running-mate/ |title=Crossroads 2026: Alan Wilson Taps S.C. Senator as Running Mate |last=Folks |first=Will |work=FITSNews |date=2026-01-09 |access-date=2026-10-15}}</ref> received 26% of the vote in the June 9, 2026 Republican primary, placing second to the 29% of [[Lieutenant Governor of South Carolina|Lt. Governor]] [[Pamela Evette]].<ref name="nbcprimary">{{cite web |title=South Carolina Governor Primary Results 2026 |work=NBC News |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-primary-elections/south-carolina-governor-results |access-date=October 11, 2026}}</ref> U.S. Representatives [[Nancy Mace]] and [[Ralph Norman]], who also ran in the primary and finished fifth and third, respectively, subsequently endorsed Wilson, as did Senator [[Tim Scott]]. Because no candidate had received a majority of the votes cast in the primary, a runoff was held on June 23, 2026, in which Wilson defeated Evette and claimed the Republican nomination.<ref name=":1" />

== Political views==
===Abortion===
A staunch [[Anti-abortion movement|opponent of abortion]], Wilson defended a state law banning most [[Abortion in South Carolina|abortions in South Carolina]] from a constitutional challenge.<ref>Meg Kinnard, [https://apnews.com/article/columbia-lawsuits-us-supreme-court-south-carolina-courts-9fab26837220890893d8f9cc8f86e471 Prosecutors seek validation of South Carolina abortion ban], Associated Press (March 2, 2021).</ref> In 2021, Wilson joined a [[U.S. Supreme Court]] filing calling on the Court to overturn ''[[Roe v. Wade]]''.<ref>Jessica Holdman, [https://www.postandcourier.com/columbia/news/sc-attorney-general-wilson-joins-in-support-of-mississippi-abortion-ban-to-supreme-court/article_c799b2dc-f167-11eb-beb1-33f6c288c8d6.html SC Attorney General Wilson joins in support of Mississippi abortion ban to Supreme Court], ''Post & Courier'' (July 30, 2021).</ref><ref>David Travis Bland, [https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/state-politics/article253149968.html SC's top prosecutor joins group to support abortion ban case in front of Supreme Court], ''The State'' (July 30, 2021).</ref>

=== Election integrity ===

==== Election-fraud claims ====
On January 12, 2012, Wilson falsely claimed on [[Fox News]] that "We found out that there were over 900 people who died and then subsequently voted. That number could be even higher than that."<ref>{{cite AV media |url-status = dead |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20130726234641/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uBEE0ZccZ-o |archive-date = 2013-07-26| url = https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uBEE0ZccZ-o |title = YouTube, a Google company |website=[[YouTube]]}}{{cbignore}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{cite news| url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/fact-checker/post/the-case-of-zombie-voters-in-south-carolina/2013/07/24/86de3c64-f403-11e2-aa2e-4088616498b4_blog.html | newspaper=The Washington Post | first=Glenn | last=Kessler | title=The case of 'zombie' voters in South Carolina | date=July 26, 2013}}</ref> These claims were untrue, and an exhaustive investigation by the [[South Carolina Law Enforcement Division]] found no evidence to support the claims of "zombie voters" in South Carolina.<ref name=":0" />

==== Efforts to challenge 2020 presidential election outcome ====
{{Main|Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election}}

In December 2020, Wilson joined a lawsuit by 16 Republican state attorneys general [[Texas v. Pennsylvania|urging the U.S. Supreme Court to overturn the results]] of the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020 presidential election]], specifically by challenging [[Joe Biden]]'s victory in four states.<ref name="APFacesComplaint">[https://apnews.com/article/election-2020-donald-trump-columbia-lawsuits-elections-1af29a09d9cbc3ae7b4d4a0bc99fd8d5 South Carolina AG faces complaint over election challenge], Associated Press (January 23, 2021).</ref><ref name="Nullify">John Monk, [https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article247734675.html SC AG Wilson joins Texas in trying to nullify Biden election], ''The State'' (December 10, 2020).</ref> The suit did not allege fraud; it questioned voting by mail in the four states and argued that their legislatures had been denied their constitutional role in overseeing it.<ref name="Nullify" /> The next month, in advance of the [[2021 United States Capitol attack|January 6 attack on the United States Capitol]] by a pro-Trump mob, the [[Republican Attorneys General Association]], then chaired by Wilson, made robocalls encouraging "patriots" to march on Washington and demand that Congress overturn the election results and keep Trump in power.<ref name="APFacesComplaint" /> Wilson later acknowledged Biden as the election's winner on January 11, 2021.<ref name="APFacesComplaint" /><ref>John Monk, [https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article248418080.html SC's AG Alan Wilson: Biden is legitimate, Pence had no power to overturn election], ''The State'' (January 11, 2021).</ref> He said he was "completely unaware" of the Republican Attorneys General Association robocalls; the group's executive director, a former Wilson aide Adam Piper, resigned.<ref name="APFacesComplaint" />

Five South Carolina attorneys subsequently filed complaints with the South Carolina Office of Disciplinary Counsel against Wilson, alleging that his participation in the Trump conspiracy lawsuit was an abuse of office that attempted to disenfranchise voters and had the effect of inflaming the subsequent protest. Wilson denied wrongdoing.<ref name="APFacesComplaint" /><ref>John Monk, [https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article248771555.html SC lawyers say SC AG Wilson participated in Trump conspiracy lawsuit, seek reprimand], ''The State'' (January 27, 2021).</ref>

=== COVID-19 pandemic ===
{{Further|COVID-19 pandemic in South Carolina}}
Wilson was one of 20 Republican state attorneys general who claimed that a portion of [[American Rescue Plan Act of 2021|President Biden's COVID-19 relief package]] (specifically, a provision that states could not redirect federal stimulus money to reduce state tax rates) was unconstitutional.<ref>Alan Benson, [https://www.postandcourier.com/columbia/sc-attorney-general-calls-portion-of-bidens-covid-19-relief-plan-unconstitutional/article_ef08f06e-8743-11eb-8251-dfb1d2fb660e.html SC Attorney General calls portion of Biden’s COVID-19 relief plan unconstitutional], ''Post & Courier'' (March 17, 2021).</ref>

In August 2021, ahead of the start of the 2021–2022 school year and amid an increasing number of COVID-19 cases in the state, Wilson said that the [[University of South Carolina]] could not implement indoor mask requirements for campus access. Wilson contended that a proviso (a type of state budget measure) passed by the General Assembly earlier that year prohibited such a mandate.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Kinnard|first=Meg|title=SC attorney general says University of South Carolina can't require masks on campus|url=https://apnews.com/article/business-health-education-coronavirus-pandemic-south-carolina-cb3af57217a3f41ecfcf636189a3c9ca|date=August 3, 2021|work=Associated Press}}</ref><ref name=MonkDaprile>John Monk & Lucas Daprile, [https://www.thestate.com/news/local/education/article253525924.html USC can require masks on campus, SC supreme court rules], ''The State'' (August 19, 2021).</ref> After Wilson's intervention, the university dropped the requirement.<ref>{{Cite news|author=Lucas Daprile & John Monk|date=August 5, 2021|title=USC reverses course after SC's top lawyer says college can't require masks indoors|work=The State|url=https://www.thestate.com/news/local/education/article253219958.html}}</ref> However, later the same month, the [[South Carolina Supreme Court]] issued a unanimous decision that rejected Wilson's interpretation,<ref>[https://law.justia.com/cases/south-carolina/supreme-court/2021/28053.html ''Creswick v. Univ. of S. Carolina''] (August 17, 2021).</ref><ref name=MonkDaprile/> and the University of South Carolina reinstated its [[Face masks during the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States#Enforcement and challenges|mask mandate]].<ref name=MonkDaprile/>

Wilson challenged the Joe Biden administration's vaccine requirement for large businesses.<ref>{{Cite news|last1=Hals|first1=Tom|last2=Scarcella|first2=Mike|date=2021-12-18|title=U.S. COVID-19 vaccine mandate revived, Supreme Court showdown looms|language=en|work=Reuters|url=https://www.reuters.com/business/healthcare-pharmaceuticals/us-appeals-court-reinstates-covid-19-vaccine-or-test-rule-us-workplaces-2021-12-18/|access-date=2021-12-18}}</ref>

=== Drug policy ===
In January 2019, Wilson described [[Cannabis (drug)|cannabis]] as "the most dangerous drug, because it is the most misunderstood drug" in the United States while denouncing legislation that would allow physicians to prescribe medical marijuana for patients.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/business/sc-police-doctors-fighting-medical-marijuana-ag-calls-it-us/article_a47ce730-1f3f-11e9-b0f8-7324237272cc.html|title=SC police, doctors fighting medical marijuana; AG calls it US's 'most dangerous drug'|author=Andrew Brown|website=Post and Courier|language=en|date=January 23, 2019}}</ref> Wilson was one of 16 state attorneys general who did not support the [[SAFE Banking Act]], which permitted the use of the banking system by cannabis-related businesses in states and territories in which cannabis is legal.<ref>[https://www.pbs.org/newshour/economy/attorneys-general-from-33-states-urge-banking-reform-for-pot-industry Attorneys general from 33 states urge banking reform for pot industry], [[Associated Press]], May 8, 2019. Retrieved June 3, 2019.</ref>

Wilson is the respondent of an ongoing lawsuit which alleges that Wilson and [[South Carolina Law Enforcement Division]] (SLED) denied a farmer due process when they destroyed his hemp farm.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-09-23 |title=South Carolina cannabis fight persists in farmer's lawsuit |url=https://apnews.com/article/lawsuits-south-carolina-agriculture-69825585e9b1b59ee558f2dfb72becc6 |access-date=2023-03-07 |website=AP News}}</ref>

===Environmental policy===
In 2021, Wilson joined a Republican lawsuit challenging President Biden's order directing federal agencies to consider the costs of [[Greenhouse gas emissions by the United States|greenhouse gas pollution]] in making decisions.<ref>Sammy Fretwell, [https://www.thestate.com/news/local/environment/article249811238.html SC attorney general joins fight against Biden's recent climate change order], ''The State'' (March 9, 2021).</ref> Wilson also joined another lawsuit challenging Biden's decision to revoke the federal permit for the [[Keystone XL Pipeline]], an oil pipeline project 1,000 miles from South Carolina.<ref>Sammy Fretwell, [https://www.thestate.com/news/local/environment/article250042884.html SC attorney general joins lawsuit against Biden over Great Plains oil pipeline], ''The State'' (March 18, 2021).</ref>

=== Healthcare ===
Wilson supported lawsuits to overturn the Affordable Care Act.<ref>{{Cite web|author=Lauren Sausser|title=S.C. Attorney General Alan Wilson joins fight to overturn Affordable Care Act|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/health/s-c-attorney-general-alan-wilson-joins-fight-to-overturn-affordable-care-act/article_e7980a2a-1c9a-11e8-a531-b339705bbb77.html|access-date=2021-08-07|website=Post and Courier|date=28 February 2018 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title='ObamaCare' Challengers Say 'This Is Not About Health Care'|url=http://abcnews.go.com/blogs/politics/2012/03/obamacare-challengers-say-this-is-not-about-health-care|access-date=2021-08-07|website=ABC News|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|agency=Associated Press|date=2018-12-15|title=South Carolina attorney general applauds ruling on health care overhaul|url=https://abcnews4.com/news/lowcountry-and-state-politics/south-carolina-attorney-general-applauds-ruling-on-health-care-overhaul|access-date=2021-08-07|via=WCIV}}</ref>

=== Immigration ===
In July 2017, Texas Attorney General [[Ken Paxton]] led a group of Republican attorneys general from nine other states, including Wilson, plus Idaho Governor [[Butch Otter]], in threatening that they would litigate against the [[Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals]] (DACA) policy that had been put into place by President [[Barack Obama]].<ref>[https://www.vox.com/policy-and-politics/2017/9/1/16243944/daca-tennessee-dream-act Tennessee’s attorney general: I’ve changed my mind, DACA is good, pass the DREAM Act], ''[[Vox.com]]'', Dara Linddara, September 1, 2017. Retrieved September 2, 2017.</ref><ref>[https://www.texastribune.org/2017/06/29/texas-leads-10-states-urging-trump-end-daca/ Texas leads 10 states in urging Trump to end Obama-era immigration program], ''[[Texas Tribune]]'', Julián Aguilar, June 29, 2017. Retrieved September 2, 2017.</ref><ref>[https://www.splcenter.org/news/2017/06/30/splc-denounces-letter-10-attorneys-general-seeking-%E2%80%98cruel-and-heartless%E2%80%99-repeal-daca SPLC denounces letter from 10 Attorneys General seeking "cruel and heartless" repeal of DACA], [[Southern Poverty Law Center]], June 30, 2017. Retrieved September 2, 2017.</ref>

=== Same-sex marriage ===
In 2014, Wilson asked the [[South Carolina Supreme Court]] to block the issuance of marriage licenses to [[Same-sex marriage in South Carolina|same-sex couples in the state]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.wcnc.com/story/news/politics/2014/10/08/attorney-gen-asks-to-halt-gay-marriage-licenses-in-sc/16937367/|title=Attorney Gen. asks to halt gay marriage licenses in SC|date=October 8, 2014|agency=Associated Press|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141015230014/http://www.wcnc.com/story/news/politics/2014/10/08/attorney-gen-asks-to-halt-gay-marriage-licenses-in-sc/16937367/ |archive-date=2014-10-15 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Wilson waged an exhaustive legal fight to block same-sex marriage in South Carolina, and in 2015, U.S. District Judge [[Richard Gergel]] ordered Wilson to pay more than $134,000 in attorneys' fees to plaintiffs who successfully challenged the state's ban on same-sex marriage.<ref>Eva Moore, [https://www.postandcourier.com/free-times/news/judge-orders-alan-wilson-to-pay-attorneys-fees-in-s-c-same-sex-marriage-case/article_55c65315-bb06-5245-9667-6bb1ce0cf30e.html Judge Orders Alan Wilson to Pay Attorneys' Fees in S.C. Same-Sex Marriage Case], ''Post & Courier'' (August 11, 2015).</ref>

== Controversies ==
===Investigation of campaign contributions===
In 2013, Wilson self-reported his campaign failed to report at least 84 contributions and expenditures on required public reports. In February 2013, Wilson originally admitted his campaign failed to disclose and report receiving at least 15 separate contributions of unknown amounts.<ref>[http://www.free-times.com/index.php?cat=1992209084141467&act=post&pid=11862002133046897] {{Webarchive|url=https://archive.today/20130410201742/http://www.free-times.com/index.php?cat=1992209084141467&act=post&pid=11862002133046897|date=2013-04-10}} "SC Attorney General Didn't Report Campaign Contributions", ''The Columbia Free Times'', February 21, 2013.</ref> A further investigation in March 2013 revealed at least 68 unreported contributions and 16 unreported expenditures. As the errors were self-reported and the reports were subsequently re-filed, Wilson faced no penalties.<ref>[http://www.thestate.com/2013/03/22/2688766/ag-wilson-campaign-reports-missing.html] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130324083648/http://www.thestate.com/2013/03/22/2688766/ag-wilson-campaign-reports-missing.html|date=2013-03-24}} "SC Attorney General discovers $134,000 in unreported contributions, expenses," ''The State Newspaper'', March 22, 2013.</ref>

===Corruption investigation ===
{{see also|South Carolina Statehouse corruption investigation}}

In late 2013, a State Law Enforcement Division report focusing on former state House Speaker [[Bobby Harrell]] was transferred to the attorney general's office. The report mentioned state representatives Rick Quinn Sr. and [[Rick Quinn Jr.]] suggesting misconduct by the younger Quinn. In early 2014, Wilson recused himself from the investigation and appointed David Pascoe, the solicitor for the 1st Judicial Circuit, as [[special prosecutor]]. Harrell pleaded guilty and resigned from the House in late 2014. Pascoe urged the attorney general's office to investigate the Quinns.<ref name=MonkThird/> After Harrell pleaded guilty to misusing campaign money and resigned from the House in late 2014, Pascoe emailed the attorney general's office, urging the Quinns be investigated.<ref name=MonkThird/> Wilson secretly recruited Quinn to assist in drafting a response letter to Pascoe, saying that his role as a special prosecutor was complete; Wilson later said that this was a mistake.<ref name=MonkThird/><ref>Glenn Smith, [https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/emails-south-carolina-ag-coordinated-with-key-figure-in-statehouse-probe-on-letter-booting-special/article_db600c26-7871-11e7-9109-7bb3a89fa640.html Emails: South Carolina AG coordinated with key figure in statehouse probe on letter booting special prosecutor off case], ''Post & Courier'' (August 5, 2017).</ref>

In March 2016, after Pascoe continued to use the state grand jury to investigate possible corruption in the General Assembly, Wilson attempted to fire Pascoe, triggering a political firestorm in the state.<ref name=MonkThird/><ref>Jeremy Borden, [https://www.postandcourier.com/free-times/news/attorney-general-tries-to-fire-special-prosecutor-sets-off-firestorm/article_63c0ceda-7866-572e-8321-483eb59d702f.html Attorney General Tries to Fire Special Prosecutor, Sets Off Firestorm], ''Post & Courier'' (May 16, 2019).</ref><ref name=CantFire>John Monk, [https://www.thestate.com/news/local/crime/article68608327.html Special prosecutor says attorney general can't fire him from corruption probe], ''The State'' (March 31, 2016).</ref> Adam Piper, a Wilson aide, launched a secret effort to smear Pascoe.<ref name=MonkThird/><ref>John Monk, [https://www.newsobserver.com/news/politics-government/article70108697.html SC attorney general's deputy secretly tried to discredit special prosecutor], ''The State'' (April 5, 2016).</ref> Pascoe challenged Wilson's attempt to fire him, arguing that the attorney general could not do so after previously recusing himself on the grounds of a conflict of interest.<ref name=CantFire/>  In July 2016, the [[South Carolina Supreme Court]] sided with Pascoe on a 4&ndash;1 decision, rejecting Wilson's attempt to fire him as special prosecutor.<ref name=MonkThird/><ref name=LosesBid>John Monk, [https://www.thestate.com/news/local/crime/article89305172.html SC Attorney General Wilson loses bid to stop Pascoe from investigating General Assembly], ''The State'' (July 16, 2016).</ref> Ultimately, the younger Rick Quinn resigned from office and pleaded guilty to a reduced charge, while the elder Quinn agreed to testify before the state grand jury, and his consulting firm pleaded guilty to failing to register as a lobbyist.<ref name=MonkThird/>

In October 2018, after a two-year investigation, a state [[grand jury]] issued a 270-page report on corruption in South Carolina, including blurred relationships between businesses, legislators, lobbyists, and political consultants. The grand jury determined that Wilson's failure to act following the guilty plea of Harrell and the Quinns impeded an investigation into state government corruption, although it made no finding as to Wilson's intent.<ref name=Collins>Jeffrey Collins, [https://apnews.com/article/bobby-harrell-elections-sc-state-wire-statutes-south-carolina-5f2aebb0c5c94931b7e82d070ee625e1 State Grand Jury: Attorney General impeded corruption probe], Associated Press (October 9, 2018).</ref> The investigation and the report focused in part on Wilson's close ties to Richard Quinn, a prominent Republican political strategist in the state, and his son Rick Quinn Jr., who had been a member of the state House.<ref name=MonkThird>John Monk, [https://www.thestate.com/news/local/crime/article211245469.html Haunted by Quinns, Alan Wilson seeks third term as SC attorney general], ''The State'' (May 18, 2018).</ref><ref>Kirk Brown, [https://www.greenvilleonline.com/story/news/2018/06/04/s-c-attorney-general-hasnt-cut-cord-controversial-quinn-family/665821002/ S.C. Attorney General Alan Wilson hasn't severed ties to controversial Quinn family], ''The Greenville News'' (June 4, 2018).</ref> Wilson had paid the elder Quinn's firm more than $220,000 for political strategy services in two election campaigns.<ref name=MonkThird/> Wilson denied wrongdoing and asserted that the 2018 grand jury's report was politically motivated.<ref name=Collins/> Judge [[Clifton Newman]] ordered that the report be released to the public.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2018-10-03 |title=Judge orders release of SC political corruption report |url=https://apnews.com/article/5f25de6419804f18abb2bdd53cd39651 |access-date=2023-03-07 |website=AP NEWS |language=en}}</ref>
[[File:White House Meeting on Protecting Consumers from Social Media Abuse (50379592573).jpg|thumb|Wilson (second from the right) with other Attorney Generals at a [[White House]] Meeting on Protecting Consumers from Social Media Abuse, 2020]]

==Personal life==
Wilson and his wife, Jennifer (née Miskewicz), a former [[WIS (TV)|WIS-TV]] news reporter, have two children.<ref name="statewide candidate biographies" /><ref>{{Cite news |date=June 11, 2006 |title=Jennifer Anne Miskewicz, Alan Wilson to marry in fall |work=New Jersey Hills Media Group |url=https://www.newjerseyhills.com/jennifer-anne-miskewicz-alan-wilson-to-marry-in-fall/article_f4c27cba-20fd-51d2-a29d-6208eab59b94.html |access-date=March 1, 2023}}</ref> Wilson joined the [[South Carolina National Guard]] in 1996 and received the [[Combat Action Badge]] for service in Iraq.<ref name="campaign about" /> Wilson's brother, Julian Wilson, is co-owner of the gun manufacturer and retailer [[Palmetto State Armory]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Holdman |first1=Jessica |title=SC's Palmetto State Armory signs tax deal for former West Columbia brake plant |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/columbia/business/scs-palmetto-state-armory-signs-tax-deal-for-former-west-columbia-brake-plant/article_40f9b654-a77b-11eb-8b3e-d7189f209ff5.html |access-date=20 September 2022 |publisher=The Post and Courier |date=27 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211110020927/https://www.postandcourier.com/columbia/business/scs-palmetto-state-armory-signs-tax-deal-for-former-west-columbia-brake-plant/article_40f9b654-a77b-11eb-8b3e-d7189f209ff5.html |archive-date=10 November 2021 |quote=The agreement was signed by the chief financial officer of JJE Capital Holdings, the private equity firm behind Palmetto State Armory helmed by co-owners Jamin McCallum, Edward Laroque and Julian Wilson, the son of U.S. Rep. Joe Wilson, R-Springdale, and brother of S.C. Attorney General Alan Wilson.}}</ref>

